why: the only real open defect left from the benchmark work. §6b says
  `flushAfter` costs 9% over the chunked merge and that a standing
  window costs nothing at all ("230.0 +-3.3 with a 30-second window
  against 230.1 +-0.9 without, the same number"). Measured 2026-09-09
  over six rounds with tight bars it is **1.23x** — 386.8 against
  314.5 — and 9% is outside those bars.

  The two lanes differ by ONE argument, `flushAfter = Some(1000)`.
  A thousand milliseconds against an operation that takes ~380
  MICROseconds: the timer never fires once. So the 23% is the cost of
  the machinery STANDING, which is exactly what the page says is free.

what: find where a never-firing timer costs 23%, then fix that.
  Profile first. Four of my predictions on this codebase have been
  wrong and every one was refuted by a measurement I could have taken
  first.

predict: none, deliberately.
  DISQUALIFYING: if the profile shows the cost is not in the flush
  path at all but in `mergeFlushing` taking a different route through
  the merge than `merge` does, then this is not a timer problem and
  the entry is re-scoped rather than fixed — the two lanes would not
  be one argument apart in anything but their signature.
